A||N||5||ur2biteme||Duke Knocking||||5||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||10-09-2000||06:23 AM||||well, it might be my engine finally showing it's age, but i noticed on one of my nighttime country drives, while going about 40 in 4th up a hill (bout 2100 rpm) it started a preignition type rattling.  it's never done this before (yeah, it's taken until OVER 170k to start) and i took the air cleaner off to check and see if there is something amiss with the fuel delivery, and voila!  leaking tbi!  <P>my question is, would a slightly leaking injector cause a slight pinging under a heavier than usual cruizing load?  i'm thinking right now that the injector being replaced would solve this, but that's why im posting.  to be sure. <P>btw, i just installed new plugs, wires are o.k.  tps, iac, o2 sensors are under a year old, egr is working properly, i run a 180 degree thermostat and no overheating problems, new DIS bricks, and the map sensor seems to be in good shape.  <P>thanks for any input<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||10-09-2000||04:49 PM||||<BR>This is just a guess, but I suppose a leaking injector could cause an engine to run rich which could lead to a carbon build-up in the combustion chambers. This in turn creates a higher compression ratio which could lead to pinging in the scenario you described.<P>My '87 duke has always pinged. Join the club... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-09-2000||06:31 PM||||Actually there is a GM TSB on that. There's an update to the PROM. Which update you need depends on which PROM you have. You need an ECM scanner to read the PROM ID. (This is for ping durring light load at around 2000rpm.) The PROM update is only available thru dealers. (TSB# 89-6-65 Multiple drivability concerns for 2.5l engine 08/04/89)<P>Before worrying about the PROM make sure there are no vac leaks. even tiny ones. Make sure the exhaust manifold hasn't cracked. (If either of your cars are 87 cyl automatic then read the article about the torque strut in my cave. It could save you whacking the hell out of the TBI.)<P>pull the plugs and check condition and gap. make sure gap is .060. DIS gets real crabby if the gaps don't all match.<P>Also try a 180 thermostat or a new 195. If the thermostat is even a little shot then it will run a few degreees warmer that it should. (I don't have the updated PROM but I use the 180 stat and I don't ping. If you live in a hot area you'll also want the radiator fan on.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||10-09-2000||06:36 PM||||Detonation has everything to do with air fuel ratios. Get the injector replaced with a rebuilt unit $60 and then diagnose your problem from there if you still have one. Your engine is not going to have the correct air fuel ratio with a faulty fuel injector.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||10-09-2000||08:23 PM||||88formula- my thoughts exactly.  just looking for confirmation on that.<P>ogre- my car is an '87 w/ 5 speed, manifold is in good shape, no vacuum leaks, running a 180* thermostat, so it seems that i've allready addressed these issues.  but thanks for the input.  didn't think much of the vacuum system causing this, but it makes sense.<P>Patrick-  i kinda thought it probably caused the pinging because it just started doing it.  it's a new phenomena for my car.  i figured the injector had something to do with it not only because of the pinging, but the constant troubles with inconsistent idle.<P>Thanks guys for the help.  as soon as i get this damn thing to pass emissions, i'll start working on it.  at least i'll have 2 more years to worry about it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000005||ur2biteme||10-09-2000||08:26 PM||||btw, i have always ran 87 octane gas in it, and have never had the pinging problem until now.  i dont want to have to pay more for gas by going to 89 or 92, i'd rather fix the problem than elimanate the symptom.<P>also, the pinging is extremely slight, and only under light-to-medium load (up a mild hill, etc.)at around 2100 rpm, maintaining speed.||||reg||1
